hume-enquiry-3.md:1: argue/answered: the conclusion: answered no, which keeps hume-enquiry-3.logic.r1 and hume-enquiry-3.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "inferences from the usual conduct of mankind attain certainty proportioned to our experience of that conduct" is true and "above one half of human reasonings contain inferences of a similar nature" is false. Is the conclusion, "Above one half of human reasonings contain inferences of a similar nature, attended with more or less degrees of certainty proportioned to our experience of the usual conduct of mankind in such particular situations.", true then?
